= Applying Refinement Types to Enforce Appointment Scheduling Constraints

== Objective

Explore how refinement types can be used to enforce correctness in appointment scheduling logic by shifting validation from runtime checks to compile-time guarantees.

== Description

Traditional systems rely on runtime validation to enforce constraints such as valid time ranges, non-overlapping appointments, and proper data formats.

This approach introduces several issues:

* Increased testing effort ("testing debt")
* Possibility of missing validation checks
* Runtime overhead
* Risk of invalid states existing during execution

An alternative is using refinement types, where constraints are encoded directly into the type system.

Instead of validating during execution:

[source,python]
----
if time < 0:
    raise ValueError("Invalid time")
----

We define constraints structurally:

[source]
----
type Time = x: int | valid_time_range(x)
----

This ensures invalid values cannot exist once accepted.

== Application to Appointment System

=== Appointment Time Constraint

[source]
----
type AppointmentTime = t: int | 0 <= t <= 1440
----

Ensures appointments fall within a valid daily range.

=== Appointment Duration Constraint

[source]
----
type Duration = d: int | d > 0
----

Prevents invalid durations.

=== Capacity Constraint

[source]
----
type Capacity = c: int | c > 0
----

Ensures valid system limits.

== Comparison with Traditional Approach

[cols="1,1"]
|===
|Runtime Validation | Refinement Types

|Checks occur during execution
|Checks enforced at compile-time

|Requires extensive testing
|Reduces testing effort

|Invalid states may exist
|Invalid states are prevented

|Higher overhead
|More efficient structure
|===

The lecture highlights that relying only on testing can lead to coverage gaps and hidden issues. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}

== Limitations

Refinement types introduce:

* Increased complexity
* Need for language/tool support
* Input validation still required at system boundaries

They complement testing rather than replace it.

== Verification Strategy

* Attempt invalid assignments (e.g., negative time)
* Confirm rejection by type system
* Compare behavior with runtime validation
* Evaluate reduction in test cases

== Conclusion

Refinement types enforce correctness by design.

They:

* Reduce testing complexity
* Prevent entire classes of bugs
* Improve reliability and maintainability

This approach shifts validation from runtime to compile-time, aligning with modern software engineering practices.
